body:
I added a more targeted workaround for this particular problem (checksum with invalid parity) and the file now shows subtitles. This is less risky than the larger parity change. I will send it for review internally and see what the team says.

The change adds this block before the `isCaptionValid` assignment near the top of `decode()`:

```java
if (isCtrlCode(ccData1) && ccData1 == 0x0F && !ODD_PARITY_BYTE_TABLE[ccByte2]) {
  // The checksum has invalid parity, so we just ignore it (but don't clear the existing
  // subtitle data).
  continue;
}
```

> ```java
> if (isCtrlCode(ccData1) && ccData1 == 0x0F && ODD_PARITY_BYTE_TABLE[ccByte2]) {
>   // The checksum has invalid parity, so we just ignore it (but don't clear the existing
>   // subtitle data).
>   continue;
> }
> ```

I tried this  change and it doesn't clear the screen on silence the way it should.

Ah - my suggestion has a typo, sorry! It should have a `!` here (edited):

```
&& !ODD_PARITY_BYTE_TABLE[ccByte2]
```

However, even with that change I still see the same behaviour you describe - the subtitles aren't cleared correctly.

Looking more closely at the stream it seems to have 4 checksum packets sent at the end of each subtitle line. The checksum is always 15. The first two packets have valid parity bits, the third one doesn't, and the fourth one does.

I tried reshuffling the code to ignore XDS packets (including all checksums) earlier (by moving this block above the validity check):
```
maybeUpdateIsInCaptionService(ccData1, ccData2);
if (!isInCaptionService) {
  // Only the Captioning service is supported. Drop all other bytes.
  continue;
}
```

It has the same symptoms for me with the stream you provided: The subtitles are not cleared when the person stops talking. I need to work out what the 'clear subtitles' signal is meant to be with this stream, and why we end up ignoring it (or maybe it's not present?).
